ewcommand{	odoinline}[1]{	odo[inline]{#1}} % ============================================================================= 	itle{Efficient Multi-Tenant LoRA Serving for Encoder Models\ via Late-Fusion Batched Matrix Multiplication} % Anonymized for double-blind review. \author{Anonymous}  egin{document} \maketitle % =============================================================================  egin{abstract} We introduce \sysname, a system for serving thousands of independently fine-tuned LoRA adapters on a single shared encoder model in a single GPU forward pass. Personalizing an NLP feature (e.g.\ text classification) for thousands of customers traditionally required one fine-tuned model per customer. LoRA \cite{hu2022lora} eliminated the \emph{storage} barrier by reducing per-customer state to a few hundred kilobytes; the remaining barrier is \emph{serving}---batching requests across customers without merging adapter weights into the base model. \sysname{} avoids weight merging by computing the base projection $W_0 x$ and the per-tenant LoRA delta $\Delta W_i x = B_i A_i x$ separately, then fusing them additively---enabling heterogeneous adapters and per-tenant logistic-regression heads to be batched via 	exttt{torch.bmm} with pre-allocated GPU buffers, requiring no custom CUDA kernels. On an NVIDIA L4 GPU with 	exttt{intfloat/multilingual-e5-small} as the base model, \sysname{} achieves 	extbf{556 samples/sec} throughput with 	extbf{5{,}000 pre-loaded adapters consuming only 2.1~GB of GPU memory}, and scales from 100 to 5{,}000 adapters with less than 16~ms additional latency---confirming $\mathcal{O}(1)$ scaling in adapter count. 	odoinline{Add one-sentence baseline comparison headline once Task \#2 (baseline experiments) is complete.} \end{abstract} % ============================================================================= \section{Introduction} \label{sec:intro} Deploying a personalized NLP feature---such as text classification---to thousands of customers is a storage and compute challenge. Maintaining a fully fine-tuned model per customer is infeasible: at $\approx$130~MB per copy, serving 10{,}000 customers would require over a terabyte of model weights. LoRA \cite{hu2022lora} resolves the \emph{storage} problem: by expressing each customer's adaptation as a low-rank update to a single shared base encoder, the per-customer footprint shrinks to $\approx$300~KB (rank~8, 12 layers)---a 400$	imes$ reduction. The remaining challenge is 	extbf{inference}: how to serve thousands of different LoRA adapters simultaneously on one GPU, at low latency, in batched mixed-tenant requests. Existing multi-tenant LoRA serving systems address this challenge only for \emph{generative decoder models}. Punica \cite{chen2024punica} introduces SGMV (Segmented Gather Matrix-Vector Multiplication), a custom CUDA kernel that batches per-adapter LoRA computations in a single launch; S-LoRA \cite{sheng2024slora} extends this with MBGMV (Multi-size Batched Gather Matrix-Vector) and a paged memory pool that supports thousands of concurrent adapters. Both systems are architected around autoregressive generation---KV-cache paging, token-by-token decode loops, and causal attention masks---and the Punica authors explicitly state that the decode stage is their sole optimization target. Neither supports bidirectional encoder attention, pooling layers, or per-tenant classification heads---leaving a critical production use case unaddressed. The only published system targeting encoder multi-adapter serving, PetS \cite{zhou2022pets}, was released before LoRA became the dominant parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) method. PetS supports adapters, BitFit, and MaskBERT but not LoRA-style low-rank matrix decompositions. Commercial serving stacks such as HuggingFace Text Embeddings Inference \cite{huggingface2023tei} offer no adapter support at all, and the multi-LoRA feature of vLLM \cite{kwon2023vllm} silently fails to load LoRA weights for encoder models (GitHub issues \#12808, \#30058, unresolved as of this submission). We present \sysname, a serving system that fills this gap. The core design principle is 	extbf{late fusion}: rather than merging a tenant's LoRA weights into the base model (which serializes batching), \sysname{} decomposes each projection into a shared base path $W_0 x$ and a per-sample LoRA delta $B_i A_i x$, computing both independently and summing the results. This decomposition enables an entire mixed-tenant batch to be processed in one forward pass---the base path runs once over all $B$ samples, and the LoRA paths run in parallel via a single 	exttt{torch.bmm} call per attention layer. Per-tenant classification heads with variable numbers of output classes are batched analogously via zero-padded BMM. \paragraph{Contributions.}  egin{itemize} \item We identify and formalize the \emph{encoder multi-tenant LoRA serving} problem, and show that existing systems leave it unsolved (\Sef{sec:relwork}). \item We present late-fusion BMM serving, an architecture that batches arbitrary mixed-tenant encoder requests without weight merging, without custom CUDA kernels, and without modification to the base model (\Sef{sec:system}). \item We demonstrate that adapter count does not drive inference latency: scaling from 100 to 5{,}000 adapters adds $<$16~ms at batch size 32 on an NVIDIA L4 GPU, while serving throughput peaks at 556~samples/sec with 5{,}000 pre-loaded adapters occupying 1.5~GB (\Sef{sec:experiments}). 	odoinline{Add contribution bullet for baseline comparison and real-task accuracy once Tasks \#2, \#3 are complete.} \item \sysname{} runs on standard PyTorch with no custom CUDA compilation, making it deployable on managed GPU environments (Databricks, SageMaker, Modal) where custom kernel builds are impractical. \end{itemize} % ============================================================================= \section{Background} \label{sec:background} \subsection{LoRA for Encoder Models} \label{sec:background_lora} LoRA \cite{hu2022lora} decomposes a weight update $\Delta W \in \mathbb{R}^{d 	imes d}$ as the product of two low-rank matrices:  egin{equation} \Delta W = B A, \quad A \in \mathbb{R}^{r 	imes d},\; B \in \mathbb{R}^{d 	imes r},\; r \ll d. \end{equation} The full forward pass for a single sample becomes $h = W_0 x +  rac{\alpha}{r} B A x$, where $W_0$ is the frozen pre-trained weight and $\alpha$ is a scaling hyperparameter typically set equal to $r$. For an encoder of hidden size $d = 384$ with $L = 12$ layers, applying LoRA at rank $r = 8$ to the query and value projections yields $2 L \cdot (d r + r d) = 147{,}456$ trainable parameters per adapter---a $24	imes$ reduction over full fine-tuning of those layers. The theoretical justification for this compression comes from \citet{aghajanyan2021intrinsic}, who showed that fine-tuning updates lie in a surprisingly low-dimensional subspace: even a random projection down to $\sim$200 dimensions retains 90\% of task performance on GLUE benchmarks. This intrinsic low dimensionality is precisely what LoRA exploits. In this work, $B$ is initialized to zero so that $\Delta W = 0$ at the start of training, leaving the pre-trained representations intact. \subsection{The Multi-Tenant Serving Problem} \label{sec:background_serving} Consider a service that exposes a single classification feature to $N$ customers, where each customer has fine-tuned 	exttt{intfloat/multilingual-e5-small} \cite{wang2024e5} on their own labeled data to specialize the feature to their domain. Without LoRA, three naive serving strategies all fail at scale: \paragraph{One model per tenant.} Storing $N$ full model copies requires $N 	imes 130$~MB for float32. At $N = 5{,}000$ that is 650~GB---the equivalent of more than eight NVIDIA A100-80GB GPUs dedicated to weight storage alone. Moreover, cross-tenant batching is impossible: different model instances cannot share a forward pass. \paragraph{Weight merging per request.} Load one base model; before each request, compute $W' = W_0 + B_i A_i$ in-place, run inference, then restore $W_0$. This approach has several fatal flaws: (i) the merge-restore cycle is \emph{serial}, blocking all other requests during weight mutation; (ii) in-place CUDA tensor modifications cause memory fragmentation and out-of-memory crashes under load; (iii) a crash between merge and restore contaminates subsequent tenants' requests with the wrong adapter---a correctness and security failure. \paragraph{Sequential single-tenant batching.} Serve all requests for tenant $i$ as a batch, then switch adapters for tenant $i+1$. Cross-tenant requests cannot be batched, so average GPU utilization approaches $1/N$---near zero for large $N$. The right solution batches \emph{heterogeneous} adapters in a single forward pass, which requires a decomposition where different linear operations can be applied per sample without serialization. % ============================================================================= \section{\sysname: System Design} \label{sec:system}  egin{figure}[t] \centering 	odo[inline,color=yellow]{Figure 1: Architecture diagram showing the late-fusion forward pass. Should depict: (left) per-sample adapter stacking into batched B/A tensors; (center) split base path W0x and delta path BAx running in parallel; (right) element-wise fusion and LR head BMM. Task \#14.} \caption{Overview of late-fusion BMM serving in \sysname. For a mixed-tenant batch of size $B$, the base attention projection $W_0 x$ runs once (shared weights), while per-sample LoRA deltas $B_i A_i x$ are computed in two 	exttt{torch.bmm} calls. All $B$ samples are fused in a single forward pass regardless of how many distinct adapters are present.} \label{fig:overview} \end{figure} \subsection{Late-Fusion Decomposition} \label{sec:lf_decomp} The core insight is that the LoRA forward pass $h = W_0 x + B_i A_i x$ can be split into two \emph{independent} computations:  egin{align} h_{	ext{base}} &= W_0 x \label{eq:base} \ h_{	ext{delta}} &= B_i (A_i x) \label{eq:delta} \ h &= h_{	ext{base}} + h_{	ext{delta}} \label{eq:fuse} \end{align} Equation~ef{eq:base} uses the same frozen $W_0$ for every sample in the batch and is computed by standard PyTorch linear layers. Equation~ef{eq:delta} applies a \emph{different} $(A_i, B_i)$ pair to each sample $i$, which requires batched matrix multiplication rather than a broadcast multiply. Equation~ef{eq:fuse} is a zero-allocation element-wise addition. For a batch of $B$ samples with hidden size $H$ and sequence length $S$:  egin{align} h_{	ext{shrink}} &= 	exttt{bmm}(x,\; [A_0,\ldots,A_{B-1}]) \in \mathbb{R}^{B 	imes S 	imes r} \label{eq:shrink} \ h_{	ext{delta}} &= 	exttt{bmm}(h_{	ext{shrink}},\; [B_0,\ldots,B_{B-1}]) \in \mathbb{R}^{B 	imes S 	imes H} \label{eq:expand} \end{align} 
oindent where $[A_0, \ldots, A_{B-1}] \in \mathbb{R}^{B 	imes H 	imes r}$ is the stack of per-sample shrink matrices, assembled from the adapter store at request time. Both 	exttt{bmm} calls are a single GPU kernel launch, processing all $B$ samples simultaneously regardless of how many distinct adapters they reference. This decomposition contrasts with the SGMV kernel used in Punica \cite{chen2024punica} along three axes. First, \emph{segmentation}: SGMV groups inputs by LoRA model ID (``We group inputs to the same LoRA model consecutively'' \cite{chen2024punica}) and dispatches one thread-block per adapter group; 	exttt{torch.bmm} applies a different $(A_i, B_i)$ to every batch item with no grouping. Second, \emph{operand shape}: SGMV operates on vector inputs $(1, H)$ optimized for the autoregressive decode shape; 	exttt{bmm} operates on the matrix shape $(S, H)$ native to encoder prefill. Third, \emph{deployability}: SGMV is a custom CUDA kernel that must be compiled against a specific CUDA version; 	exttt{bmm} ships with PyTorch and runs anywhere PyTorch runs. S-LoRA \cite{sheng2024slora} extends Punica with prefill-shaped variants (MBGMM/MBGMV) that handle non-contiguous adapter memory, but the kernels still target autoregressive LLM serving with a KV cache and do not expose the encoder operations (pooling, classification heads) needed for classification workloads. \subsection{Batched Classification Heads} \label{sec:lr_head} In the multi-tenant classification setting, each tenant trains a logistic-regression (LR) head on top of the encoder's pooled 	exttt{[CLS]} representation. Tenants differ in the number of output classes: tenant $i$ may have $c_i$ classes where $c_i$ varies from 2 to over 100. \sysname{} serves all heads in a single BMM call. Given a batch of pooled representations $p \in \mathbb{R}^{B 	imes 1 	imes H}$ and per-tenant weight matrices $W^{	ext{cls}}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{c_i 	imes H}$ and bias vectors $b^{	ext{cls}}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{c_i}$, we zero-pad all weight matrices to the maximum label count $c_{\max} = \max_i c_i$ in the batch and form a stacked tensor $W^{	ext{cls}} \in \mathbb{R}^{B 	imes c_{\max} 	imes H}$. The logit computation is:  egin{equation} 	ext{logits} = 	exttt{bmm}(p,\; {W^{	ext{cls}}}^	op) + b^{	ext{cls}} \;\in\; \mathbb{R}^{B 	imes 1 	imes c_{\max}} \end{equation} Callers slice $[:, :, :c_i]$ per sample before applying softmax. The zero-padded columns contribute zero gradient and do not affect the probability distribution of any tenant. \subsection{Pre-Allocated GPU Buffers} \label{sec:buffers} Without explicit buffer management, 	exttt{torch.bmm} allocates a new output tensor on every call. For a 12-layer encoder with target modules $\{	ext{query}, 	ext{value}\}$, this produces $2 	imes 2 	imes 12 = 48$ CUDA allocations per forward pass. Each allocation invokes the CUDA memory allocator, which searches for a free contiguous block at a cost of $\sim$10~$\mu$s per call---totaling $\sim$0.5~ms of pure allocator overhead per batch. \sysname{} pre-allocates two fixed-size buffers at initialization:  egin{align} \mathtt{out\_A} &\in \mathbb{R}^{B_{\max} 	imes S_{\max} 	imes r} \ \mathtt{out\_B} &\in \mathbb{R}^{B_{\max} 	imes S_{\max} 	imes H} \end{align} and passes them via the 	exttt{out=} argument of every 	exttt{bmm} call. This eliminates all 48 allocations per forward pass, replacing dynamic memory management with zero-overhead in-place writes. The buffers are shared across all layers because each 	exttt{expand} call immediately consumes 	exttt{out\_A} before the next 	exttt{shrink} call overwrites it. \subsection{Adapter Store} \label{sec:adapter_store} At service startup, \sysname{} pre-loads all tenant LoRA adapters into GPU memory as an 	exttt{AdapterStore}: a dictionary mapping adapter IDs to 	exttt{LoraWeight} objects, each holding tensors $W_A \in \mathbb{R}^{L 	imes H 	imes r}$ and $W_B \in \mathbb{R}^{L 	imes r 	imes H}$ where $L$ is the number of transformer layers. Matrices are stored in inference-transposed layout ($H 	imes r$ rather than $r 	imes H$ for $A$) so that 	exttt{bmm} requires no runtime transpose. For a batch of adapter IDs, the 	exttt{BatchAssembler} iterates over the batch, slices each adapter's per-layer tensor, and 	exttt{unsqueeze(0)}-es it to add a batch dimension. After all samples are processed, 	exttt{torch.cat} concatenates the per-layer lists into $(B, H, r)$ tensors ready for 	exttt{bmm}. This CPU-side assembly is the only per-request overhead before the GPU forward pass (\Sef{sec:results_latency}). \subsection{Complexity Analysis} \label{sec:complexity} \paragraph{Memory.} The base model requires $\mathcal{O}(d^2 L)$ memory regardless of tenant count. Each adapter requires $\mathcal{O}(r d L)$ memory. For $N$ tenants: total GPU memory $= \mathcal{O}(d^2 L + N r d L)$. At $d=384$, $L=12$, $r=8$: 100 adapters occupy 29~MB; 5{,}000 adapters occupy 1.47~GB. An NVIDIA L4 (23~GB) can hold $\approx$16{,}000 adapters at rank 8 before exhausting memory. \paragraph{Inference time.} The latency of a single forward pass is independent of $N$ (adapter count): it depends only on batch size $B$, sequence length $S$, and model depth $L$. The adapter count affects only CPU-side batch assembly time, which is $\mathcal{O}(B \cdot L \cdot |\mathcal{M}|)$ where $|\mathcal{M}|$ is the number of target modules. Empirically, increasing $N$ from 100 to 5{,}000 adds $<$16~ms at batch size 32---less than 2$	imes$ the baseline latency and entirely attributable to batch assembly, not GPU compute (\Sef{sec:results_latency}). % ============================================================================= \section{Experiments} \label{sec:experiments} \subsection{Setup} \label{sec:setup} \paragraph{Base model.} All primary experiments use 	exttt{intfloat/multilingual-e5-small} \cite{wang2024e5}, a 12-layer BERT-family encoder with hidden size $d=384$, 6 attention heads, and $\approx$33M parameters. 	odoinline{Add multi-model experiments (BERT-base, RoBERTa-base, mE5-large) for Task \#4. Report at least 3 base models.} \paragraph{LoRA configuration.} We apply LoRA to the query and value projection matrices of each attention layer (	exttt{target\_modules = [query, value]}). We sweep rank $r \in \{8, 16, 32\}$. \paragraph{Hardware.} Primary experiments run on a single NVIDIA L4 GPU (23~GB VRAM, \$0.39/hr on RunPod) with PyTorch 2.2 and CUDA 12.2 in float32 precision. 	odoinline{Add A100-40/80, A10, T4 sweep for Task \#4. Report throughput and memory-efficiency across GPU classes.} \paragraph{Benchmark protocol.} We measure end-to-end latency (batch assembly + encoder forward pass + LR head) using 50 warm-up iterations followed by 200 measured iterations. We sweep adapter count $N \in \{100, 500, 1000, 5000\}$, batch size $B \in \{16, 32, 64, 128\}$, and rank $r \in \{8, 16, 32\}$ for a total of 48 configurations. Each configuration samples mixed-tenant batches uniformly at random from the adapter pool. 	odoinline{Re-run with $\geq$5 random seeds and report mean $\pm$ std and 95\% CI for all metrics (Task \#6). Current numbers are single-seed.} \paragraph{Adapters.} 	odoinline{Replace synthetic adapters with real fine-tuned adapters (GLUE / MASSIVE / Banking77) for Task \#3. Current results use randomly initialized adapters with $B=0$ (delta $= 0$), which isolates serving overhead but does not validate task accuracy.} \paragraph{Baselines.} 	odoinline{Implement and report the following baselines for Task \#2: (1)~HuggingFace PEFT sequential serving---load one adapter at a time, batch intra-tenant requests, serialize across tenants; (2)~weight-merging per request---merge $W' = W_0 + B_i A_i$ before each request, un-merge after; (3)~vLLM multi-LoRA on encoders---document failure mode if applicable; (4)~LoRAX encoder mode---document whether heterogeneous batching is supported. Present a unified latency / throughput / memory table.} \subsection{Latency and Throughput Scaling} \label{sec:results_latency} Table~ef{tab:main_results} reports p50/p90/p99 latency and throughput across the 48-configuration sweep.  egin{table}[t] \centering \small \caption{Serving latency and throughput on NVIDIA L4 with 	exttt{multilingual-e5-small}, sequence length 128. All adapters are pre-loaded; latency includes batch assembly and LR head.} \label{tab:main_results}  egin{tabular}{rrr rrr r r} 	oprule $N$ & $B$ & $r$ & p50 & p90 & p99 & QPS & GPU \ & & & (ms)& (ms)& (ms)& (s/s) & (GB) \ \midrule 100 & 32 & 8 & 40.0 & 40.4 & 42.1 & 798 & 0.61 \ 500 & 32 & 8 & 59.4 & 60.5 & 61.1 & 556 & 0.73 \ 1000 & 32 & 8 & 59.6 & 66.1 & 79.6 & 549 & 0.89 \ 5000 & 32 & 8 & 	extbf{56.3} & 67.4 & 87.2 & 	extbf{556} & 2.13 \ \midrule 5000 & 16 & 8 & 33.9 & 35.9 & 36.8 & 488 & 2.08 \ 5000 & 64 & 8 & 118.9 & 140.5 & 188.6 & 523 & 2.23 \ 5000 & 128 & 8 & 263.3 & 311.0 & 400.5 & 478 & 2.42 \ \midrule 5000 & 32 & 16 & 54.5 & 65.0 & 76.4 & 572 & 3.61 \ 5000 & 32 & 32 & 59.8 & 61.8 & 70.2 & 548 & 6.56 \  ottomrule \end{tabular} \end{table} \paragraph{Finding 1: Adapter count does not drive latency.} Scaling from 100 to 5{,}000 adapters at batch size 32, rank 8 increases p50 latency by only 16~ms (40.0 $	o$ 56.3~ms)---a 40\% increase for a 50$	imes$ increase in the number of served customers. Latency at 1{,}000 and 5{,}000 adapters is nearly identical (59.6 vs.\ 56.3~ms), confirming that adapter pool size affects only CPU-side batch assembly, not GPU forward pass time. This validates the $\mathcal{O}(1)$ adapter scaling property of \Sef{sec:complexity}. \paragraph{Finding 2: Batch size is the primary latency driver.} Latency scales roughly linearly with batch size at $\approx$2~ms per sample at low adapter counts. Throughput peaks at batch size 32 ($\approx$556~samples/sec for 5{,}000 adapters), beyond which increasing batch size improves utilization but raises queue latency enough to reduce net throughput. \paragraph{Finding 3: LoRA rank has negligible latency impact.} Doubling rank from 8 to 32 adds less than 4~ms at 5{,}000 adapters and batch size 32 (56.3 $	o$ 59.8~ms). The BMM operations are memory-bandwidth bound, not compute bound, at the hidden dimensions used by small encoders. The primary cost of higher rank is GPU memory: rank 32 at 5{,}000 adapters consumes 5.9~GB for the adapter cache, versus 1.47~GB at rank 8. \paragraph{Finding 4: Predictable memory footprint.} GPU memory follows $\approx 0.53 + N \cdot 2 \cdot L \cdot H \cdot r \cdot 4 	ext{ bytes}$~GB. At rank 8, the L4's 23~GB allows $\approx$16{,}000 pre-loaded adapters. An A100-80GB could hold $\approx$55{,}000 adapters at rank 8. \paragraph{Finding 5: Tail latency widens at large batches.} The p99/p50 ratio at batch size 16 is only 1.09$	imes$; at batch size 128 it reaches 1.52$	imes$. Occasional stragglers in CPU batch assembly cause p99 spikes. For production systems with strict p99 SLAs, batch size 16--32 with continuous batching is the recommended operating point.  egin{figure}[t] 	odo[inline,color=yellow]{Figure 2: Line plot of p50 latency vs. adapter count for $B \in \{16, 32, 64, 128\}$, rank=8. Should show near-flat curves confirming O(1) adapter scaling. Generate using data in docs/benchmark\_results.csv. Task \#14.} \caption{p50 latency as a function of pre-loaded adapter count at four batch sizes (rank = 8, sequence length = 128).} \label{fig:latency_adapters} \end{figure}  egin{figure}[t] 	odo[inline,color=yellow]{Figure 3: Grouped bar chart of throughput (samples/sec) for ours vs. baselines (PEFT sequential, weight-merging). Task \#2 + Task \#14.} \caption{Throughput comparison between \sysname{} and baseline serving strategies at batch size 32, rank 8, 1{,}000 adapters.} \label{fig:throughput_baselines} \end{figure} \subsection{Comparison with Baselines} \label{sec:results_baselines} 	odoinline{Task \#2: Implement baselines (PEFT sequential, weight-merge/unmerge, vLLM multi-LoRA on encoders, LoRAX) and fill in Table~ef{tab:baselines}. Expected: PEFT sequential gives effective batch=1 for mixed-tenant workloads ($\sim$30$	imes$ lower throughput); weight-merging adds $\sim$5--10~ms overhead per request and also cannot batch cross-tenant requests.}  egin{table}[h] \centering \small \caption{Latency and throughput comparison against baseline serving strategies (5{,}000 adapters, batch=32, rank=8, sequence length=128). 	extbf{TODO}: fill in after Task \#2.} \label{tab:baselines}  egin{tabular}{lrrl} 	oprule System & p50 (ms) & QPS & Notes \ \midrule PEFT sequential & \emph{?} & \emph{?} & adapter swap per tenant group \ Weight-merge/unmerge & \emph{?} & \emph{?} & in-place $W_0 {+} BA$ per request \ vLLM multi-LoRA & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\emph{encoder LoRA broken}} & GH \#12808 \ LoRAX & \emph{?} & \emph{?} & TBD: encoder support unconfirmed \ \sysname{} (ours) & 	extbf{56.3} & 	extbf{556} & 5K adapters, B=32, r=8 \  ottomrule \end{tabular} \end{table} \subsection{Real-Task Accuracy Validation} \label{sec:results_accuracy} 	odoinline{This section requires Task \#3 (real adapter training) and Task \#9 (real adapter load path validation). Planned experiments: 
oindent	extbf{Correctness check.} Load PEFT-trained adapters from disk via 	exttt{store.load\_from\_file()}, run inference through \sysname{}, and compare against the PEFT-merged reference output using 	exttt{np.allclose(..., atol=1e-5)}. Confirm correctness across all samples. 
oindent	extbf{Task accuracy.} Train one LoRA adapter per task/domain on a multi-tenant suite such as GLUE (8 tasks), MASSIVE (intent classification, multiple locales), or Banking77 (topic classification). Report classification accuracy under \sysname{} serving versus PEFT-merged baseline. Expected: accuracy matches to within floating-point precision since the computation is mathematically equivalent. 
oindent	extbf{Scale.} Report latency with real adapters to confirm that non-zero LoRA deltas do not significantly alter the timing profile versus the synthetic-adapter benchmark. } \subsection{Multi-Model and Multi-Hardware Generalization} \label{sec:results_generalization} 	odoinline{This section requires Task \#4. Run the latency/throughput sweep on at least 3 base encoders $	imes$ 3 GPU classes: 
