ol and two Triton-implemented kernels: MBGMM (Multi-size Batched Gather Matrix-Matrix Multiplication) for the autoregressive prefill stage and MBGMV (Multi-size Batched Gather Matrix-Vector Multiplication) for the decode stage, both supporting heterogeneous LoRA ranks within a batch. Although MBGMM operates on matrix-shaped inputs, the surrounding system is built around the autoregressive prefill$	o$decode pipeline---it does not expose the encoder operations (bidirectional attention, pooling, classification heads) required for classification workloads, and never returns control to the caller between prefill and decode. \sysname{} addresses the encoder-only setting that the Punica and S-LoRA designs do not target. \paragraph{vLLM.} vLLM \cite{kwon2023vllm} introduced PagedAttention for memory-efficient LLM serving and subsequently integrated Punica/S-LoRA kernels as its multi-LoRA backend. LoRA support for embedding and pooling models is broken at the time of this submission: LoRA weights are silently not loaded for encoder models (GitHub issues \#12808, \#30058), and no timeline for a fix has been announced. \paragraph{LoRAX.} LoRAX \cite{predibase2023lorax} (Predibase) supports heterogeneous continuous batching of different LoRA adapters for generative LLMs. The system is designed around decoder-only models; whether it supports heterogeneous multi-adapter batching for encoder classifiers has not been confirmed in its documentation or benchmarks. \paragraph{HuggingFace Text Embeddings Inference.} TEI \cite{huggingface2023tei} is HuggingFace's production serving stack for encoder/embedding models (BERT, XLM-R, E5-family, ModernBERT). It achieves high throughput for single-model serving via Flash Attention and cuBLASLt, but has no adapter support whatsoever: all tenants must share identical model weights. \paragraph{PetS.} PetS \cite{zhou2022pets} is the only prior system that directly targets multi-adapter serving for encoder models. It supports Adapter \cite{PLACEHOLDER_houlsby2019}, BitFit \cite{PLACEHOLDER_bitfit2022}, and MaskBERT \cite{PLACEHOLDER_maskbert2020} operating on BERT and DistilBERT. However, PetS predates LoRA as the dominant PEFT paradigm and does not support low-rank matrix decompositions. Its architecture is also designed for a limited set of tenants and does not address the per-tenant classification head problem. \sysname{} can be seen as a LoRA-native successor to PetS's core idea, extended with variable-head batching and a pre-loaded adapter cache. \paragraph{Jina-embeddings-v3.} \citet{gunther2024jina} ship a multilingual encoder with five task-specific LoRA adapters baked in, enabling mixed-adapter batching at inference time. This demonstrates that per-sample adapter selection is feasible---but the approach is a \emph{model design} rather than a serving framework: adapters are fixed at training time and cannot be extended with new tenants without retraining. \sysname{} serves arbitrary external adapters without any modification to the base model. \paragraph{Other recent multi-LoRA systems.} MixLoRA \cite{PLACEHOLDER_mixlora2024} uses CUDA streams to overlap LoRA delta computations for LLMs; CaraServe \cite{su2023caraserve} exploits CPU memory for rank-aware adapter scheduling; dLoRA \cite{wu2024dlora} dynamically orchestrates adapter placement for generative LLM serving. All target decoder-only autoregressive inference. None address encoder classifiers, variable classification heads, or deployments without custom CUDA compilation. % ============================================================================= \section{Limitations} \label{sec:limitations} \paragraph{Uniform rank constraint.} All adapters must share the same rank $r$ and the same set of target modules. A production system serving tenants with heterogeneous fine-tuning strategies would need padding to the maximum rank in the batch, wasting compute for low-rank adapters. We demonstrate that a padded-rank approach is feasible (\Sef{sec:ablation}), 	odoinline{Complete Task \#8 (mixed-rank experiment) before finalizing this paragraph.} but a masked-BMM variant that avoids padding overhead remains future work. \paragraph{Pre-loaded adapter cache.} \sysname{} holds all adapters in GPU memory permanently. For workloads with more than $\sim$16{,}000 adapters at rank 8 on an L4, an LRU eviction policy with CPU offload would be required. Paged adapter storage analogous to PagedAttention \cite{kwon2023vllm} is a natural extension. \paragraph{CPU-side batch assembly.} The 	exttt{BatchAssembler} runs on CPU and involves Python loops with 	exttt{torch.cat}. For very high request rates ($>$10{,}000 requests/sec), this becomes the throughput bottleneck. A custom CUDA or C++ gather kernel that indexes directly into the adapter store would eliminate this overhead, at the cost of departing from pure-PyTorch deployability. \paragraph{Single-seed evaluation.} Current benchmarks use a single random seed. Statistical significance is not yet established. 	odoinline{Complete Task \#6 (multi-seed CI runs) before camera-ready.} \paragraph{Synthetic-only adapter validation.} Performance numbers are obtained with randomly initialized adapters ($B = 0$, so the LoRA delta is zero). Real adapters with non-zero deltas will have marginally higher GPU compute time; correctness against PEFT-merged references has not been verified end-to-end. 	odoinline{Complete Tasks \#3 and \#9 before camera-ready.} % ============================================================================= \section{Conclusion} \label{sec:conclusion} We presented \sysname, a system for multi-tenant LoRA serving on encoder models that fills a gap left by all existing systems: none support heterogeneous batching of different LoRA adapters in a single encoder forward pass. \sysname{} achieves this through late-fusion batched matrix multiplication---a decomposition that separates the frozen base-model path from the per-tenant LoRA delta path and fuses them additively, enabling mixed-tenant batches without weight merging, custom CUDA kernels, or KV-cache machinery. On an NVIDIA L4 GPU with 	exttt{multilingual-e5-small}, \sysname{} serves 5{,}000 pre-loaded adapters at 556~samples/sec in 56~ms p50 latency, with sub-linear growth in latency as adapter count increases 50-fold. \sysname{} requires only standard PyTorch, making it immediately deployable on managed cloud GPU environments where custom kernel compilation is unavailable---the majority of production NLP serving infrastructure today. % ============================================================================= \section*{Limitations} See \Sef{sec:limitations} for a detailed discussion. Key open items before camera-ready submission: multi-seed statistical validation (Task~\#6), baseline comparisons (Task~\#2), real-task accuracy on downstream benchmarks (Task~\#3), multi-GPU generalization (Task~\#4), and ablation study (Task~\#7). % ============================================================================= \section*{Ethics Statement} This work addresses inference-time efficiency for multi-tenant NLP systems. We identify no specific ethical concerns beyond those inherent to the underlying models (\eg bias in pre-trained encoders). The system is intended for legitimate multi-tenant SaaS deployments. Adapter isolation is enforced by design: per-tenant weights are never merged into the shared base model, eliminating cross-tenant contamination risks present in weight-merging approaches. % =============================================================================  ibliography{references} \appendix % ============================================================================= \section{Full Benchmark Results} \label{app:full_results} Table~ef{tab:full_results} reports all 48 benchmark configurations.  egin{table*}[t] \centering \small \caption{Complete benchmark sweep: 4 adapter counts $	imes$ 4 batch sizes $	imes$ 3 LoRA ranks on NVIDIA L4, sequence length 128, float32.